            "count": 0,
            "bug_id": 19320,
            "text": "The internal generics (`$`, `[[`, `[`, length(), ...) dispatch on any object with a class attribute: tryDispatch() and DispatchOrEval() create promises, an environment and a context before usemethod() looks for a method. Most classed objects have none (a data frame has no `$` method), so that set-up is discarded.\n\nTimings: internal-dispatch-bench.R, attached, prints ns per evaluation. On aarch64 macOS (Apple clang 21, r90888), o[[10L]] on a classed list goes from 136 to 38 ns, ec$k on a classed environment from 204 to 92, and df$w from 187 to 93; a dispatch that finds a method is within 2% either way.\n\nProposed patch: attached as internal-dispatch-lookup-first.diff, against r90888. The method is looked up first and passed on to the dispatch, and method names are probed in the symbol table without being created: no symbol, no binding, no method (a user-defined database on the lookup path disables the probe). Two visible differences: symbols for missing methods are no longer created, and a promise or active binding standing in for a method is evaluated before the dispatch context exists, as for group generics. It conflicts with the patch in bug 19315 in objects.c; I will rebase whichever lands second.\n\nmake check-all passes in CI on Linux, macOS and Windows. The patch is also submitted as https://github.com/r-devel/r-svn/pull/496, with the full measurements."
          },
